/* Interfaces under load.

   Three animals from the fire story, each crossing to the island its own
   way, unified by the crosser interface; hopper and a herald share the
   brace-less single-method announcer interface. The thunderbird satisfies
   its contract partly through an unto method.

   Methods return values rather than mutating, so value semantics never
   blur the polymorphism.

   ★ AN INTERFACE IS A PARAMETER TYPE AND NOTHING ELSE. It cannot be the
   element type of a container, a field, or a return type. That is what
   makes it free: the concrete type is known at every call site, so each
   conformer gets its own specialised copy of the function — no vtables,
   no type tags, no dispatch at runtime.

   So a mixed crew is held as a CLOSED UNION, and Judge narrows each
   element back to a concrete type before the call. The union says which
   animals exist; the interface says what they must be able to do. Writing
   `a catalogue with (...)` instead infers an OPEN union from the values
   and the call is refused — correctly, since an open union has no fixed
   set of conformers to specialise for. */

Define crosser as an interface for {
    The text function crossing-style,
    The number function crossing-cost, given (the number distance)
}.

Define announcer as an interface for the text function announce.

Define object hopper with (the text name) and crosser and announcer:
    Bind text to crossing-style:
        Return "swims, then dances close to the fire".
    Done.
    Bind number to crossing-cost, given (the number distance):
        Return distance * 1.
    Done.
    Bind text to announce:
        Return "I can run and swim faster than the Weasels".
    Done.
Done.

Define object thunderbird with (the text name) and crosser:
    Bind text to crossing-style:
        Return "flies through the storm".
    Done.
Done.

Bind number to crossing-cost unto thunderbird, given (the number distance):
    Return distance * 3.
Done.

Define object beaver with (the text name) and crosser:
    Bind text to crossing-style:
        Return "dams the water and walks across".
    Done.
    Bind number to crossing-cost, given (the number distance):
        Return distance * 2.
    Done.
Done.

Define object herald with (the text office) and announcer:
    Bind text to announce:
        Return one's office joined to ": the council is called".
    Done.
Done.

Bind text to report-crossing, given (the crosser who, the number distance):
    Define style as cast crossing-style on (who).
    Define cost as cast crossing-cost on (who, distance).
    Return style joined to " — " joined to cost converted to text.
Done.

Bind text to herald-line, given (the announcer who):
    Return "herald-line: " joined to cast announce on (who).
Done.

Bind void to play:
    Define trickster as a new hopper { the name "Hopper" }.
    Define crew as a catalogue of (hopper or thunderbird or beaver) with (
        trickster,
        a new thunderbird { the name "Thunderbird" },
        a new beaver { the name "Beaver" }).
    Define distance as 12.
    /* Judge narrows `it` to one concrete animal per arm, which is what
       report-crossing needs — the arms look repetitive because each one
       compiles to a different specialisation. */
    For each who in crew, repeat:
        Judge who, where it is:
            A hopper, State cast report-crossing on (it, distance).
            A thunderbird, State cast report-crossing on (it, distance).
            A beaver, State cast report-crossing on (it, distance).
        Done.
    Done.
    State "".
    State cast herald-line on (trickster).
    Define crier as a new herald { the office "town crier" }.
    State cast herald-line on (crier).
Done.

Cast play.